    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Using the Sine Rule to Find a Side</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In triangle <m>ABC</m>, <m>A=40°</m>, <m>B=65°</m> and <m>a=8</m>. Use the sine rule to find the length of side <m>b</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>First find <m>C=180°-40°-65°=75°</m>. Then use <m>\frac{a}{\sin A}=\frac{b}{\sin B}</m> so <m>b=8\frac{\sin65°}{\sin40°}\approx 12.8</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Finding an Angle with the Sine Rule</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In triangle <m>ABC</m>, side <m>a=10</m>, side <m>b=14</m> and angle <m>A=35°</m>. Find angle <m>B</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Use <m>\frac{a}{\sin A}=\frac{b}{\sin B}</m>. Then <m>\sin B=\frac{14\sin35°}{10}\approx0.803</m>, so <m>B\approx53.4°</m>.
            <br/>
            <p>The third angle is <m>C=180°-35°-53.4°\approx91.6°</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Ambiguous Case: Two Possible Angles</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In triangle <m>ABC</m>, sides <m>a=7</m>, <m>b=10</m> and angle <m>A=30°</m>. Use the sine rule to determine possible values of <m>B</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Compute <m>\sin B=\frac{10\sin30°}{7}=\frac{10\cdot0.5}{7}\approx0.714</m>. Then <m>B\approx45.5°</m> or <m>B=180°-45.5°=134.5°</m>.
            <br/>
            <p>Check each case for a valid third angle: if <m>B=45.5°</m>, <m>C\approx104.5°</m>; if <m>B=134.5°</m>, <m>C\approx15.5°</m>. Both are possible.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Solving a Side in an Obtuse Triangle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In triangle <m>ABC</m>, <m>A=110°</m>, <m>B=25°</m> and <m>a=12</m>. Find side <m>b</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Find <m>C=45°</m>. Use <m>b=12\frac{\sin25°}{\sin110°}\approx5.4</m>. The sine rule works for the obtuse angle by using the known angle measures directly.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Using the Sine Rule to Check Triangle Possibility</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Given triangle <m>ABC</m> with <m>A=50°</m>, <m>a=9</m> and <m>b=16</m>, determine whether a triangle exists, and if so find <m>B</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Compute <m>\sin B=\frac{16\sin50°}{9}\approx1.33</m>. Since the sine value is greater than 1, no triangle is possible with these values.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

